TAR(1)                    UC.OS Manual                    TAR(1)

NAME
       tar - create, list and extract tape archives

SYNOPSIS
       tar -c [-vz] [-f file] [-C dir] path...
       tar -t [-v] [-f file] [member...]
       tar -x [-vkmOp] [-f file] [-C dir] [member...]

DESCRIPTION
       tar packs files and directories into one archive, lists an
       archive, or unpacks it. Without -f it reads standard input or
       writes standard output. The old form without a dash works too:

              $ tar czf notes.tar.gz notes
              $ tar tvf notes.tar.gz
              $ tar xf notes.tar.gz -C /tmp

       When reading, gzip, xz and zstd compression is found by itself.
       Archives in the ustar, GNU and pax formats are understood,
       including long names. Archives are written in the GNU format.

OPTIONS
       -c, -t, -x
              Create, list or extract.
       -f FILE
              The archive. - means standard input or output.
       -v     Name each file. Twice: long listing.
       -z     gzip. -J (xz) and --zstd insist on those formats when
              reading; this version cannot write them.
       -a     Choose the compression from the archive's name.
       -C DIR Change to DIR first.
       -k     Do not replace existing files.
       -m     Do not restore modification times.
       -O     Extract file contents to standard output.
       -p     Restore permission bits exactly.
       --strip-components=N
              Drop the first N parts of each name.
       --owner=ID, --group=ID, --numeric-owner
              Owner and group to record when creating.
       --strict
              Exit with status 2 if links, owners or permissions could
              not be restored.

NOTES
       The kernel does not have symbolic links, hard links, chmod or
       chown yet. tar says so once for each kind and goes on. A hard
       link is replaced by a copy of the file it points to.

       Names with a .. part are skipped, and a leading / is removed.

SEE ALSO
       gzip(1)
